I’ll inspect the diffraction data and metadata first, then identify candidate phases, build/refine them in GSAS-II, and validate that the exported CIFs, phase fractions, profile, and project all agree. I’ll flag any ambiguity rather than forcing an over-specific assignment.

The chemistry points first to zinc tungstate, but I’m treating that only as a hypothesis. I’m now extracting the observed peak set and comparing it against the local crystallographic database, including checks for unreacted ZnO/WO₃ and plausible tungsten-oxide polymorphs.

All major observed lines are accounted for by three specific structures: monoclinic wolframite ZnWO₄ (P2/c), monoclinic γ-WO₃ (P2₁/c), and wurtzite ZnO (P6₃mc). I’m moving into quantitative refinement now; the key uncertainty will be separating instrumental broadening from phase-specific crystallite broadening because no calibrated profile was supplied.

The first quantitative pass reaches a low profile residual, but it exposed a GSAS-II scale redundancy and overparameterized two negligible size terms. I’m tightening the model now—removing that redundancy and comparing size versus microstrain broadening for ZnWO₄—so the reported fractions come from a stable covariance matrix rather than merely the lowest residual.

The tightened model is now stable: no dropped parameters or Hessian singularity, full-pattern \(R_{wp}=3.54\%\), and the normalized fractions settle at 72.72% ZnWO₄, 17.48% WO₃, and 9.80% ZnO. I’m doing the final consistency checks now—CIF/project agreement, residual-peak search, JSON sums, and file validation—before writing the uncertainty note.

Completed the GSAS-II refinement and validated all outputs.

| Crystalline phase | Space group | Weight fraction |
|---|---|---:|
| ZnWO₄ wolframite | P2/c | 72.72 wt% |
| γ-WO₃ | P2₁/n | 17.48 wt% |
| ZnO wurtzite | P6₃mc | 9.80 wt% |

Fit statistics: \(R_{wp}=3.537\%\), \(R_p=2.715\%\), GOF = 1.282. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline material; systematic uncertainty from uncalibrated broadening and microabsorption exceeds the formal errors.

